---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Squat One Rep Max Calculator — Estimate Your Squat 1RM"
  description="Estimate your back squat one rep max from a set of 1–12 reps. See six formula estimates, why squat depth changes the number, and loadable training weights."
  path="/squat-calculator/"
  name="Squat Calculator"
  h1="Squat One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your back squat max from a recent heavy set. The number is only as good as the reps behind it — consistent depth matters more here than on any other lift."
  exercise="squat"
  lift="Squat"
  example={{ weight: 225, reps: 5, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.warmup, LINKS.trainingMax, LINKS.deadlift, LINKS.dots]}
  sources={['lesuer', 'hprc', 'nsca']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="squat-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="squat-estimate-heading">How to estimate your squat max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Squat a weight you can move for 3–6 reps at your normal depth, stop before form breaks down,
      and enter the weight and reps above. For example, 225 lb × 5 estimates a squat max of roughly
      253–268 lb, with an Average Estimate near 260 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      The squat was one of the three lifts in a classic comparison of seven 1RM equations
      against tested maxes in college students (LeSuer et al., 1997), alongside the bench press and
      deadlift. Predictions were strongly correlated with tested maxes — but a strong correlation is
      not the same as an exact match, and individual lifters can still land well above or below any
      formula.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="squat-depth-heading">
    <h2 id="squat-depth-heading">Depth changes the number</h2>
    <p>
      A squat that stops a few inches higher is a much easier lift. If your 5-rep set was cut short
      but your max attempt would be to full depth, the estimate will read high — sometimes by a lot.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Use the same depth for every rep</strong> and for any max you compare against. Powerlifting rules require the crease of the hip to drop below the top of the knee.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep the setup consistent:</strong> the same bar position (high- or low-bar), stance and equipment, such as a belt or knee sleeves, as the max you want to predict.</li>
      <li><strong>Count only reps you would show a judge</strong> — no half reps at the end of the set.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="squat-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="squat-safety-heading">Squat safety for heavy sets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Squat inside a power rack with the safety pins or straps set just below your lowest position, so a missed rep can be set down.</li>
      <li>If you use spotters for a max attempt, they should know the plan before you unrack.</li>
      <li>Learn to bail before you need to: practise sitting a light failed rep onto the safeties.</li>
      <li>Warm up gradually — the <a href="/warm-up-calculator/">warm-up calculator</a> builds a ramp with loadable weights.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="squat-use-heading">
    <h2 id="squat-use-heading">Using your squat estimate in training</h2>
    <p>
      Squat programs often prescribe percentages such as 75–85% for sets of 3–6. Plan those from
      the Average Estimate or slightly below; an estimate from a set with shallow reps will make every
      percentage too heavy. When in doubt, re-estimate from a fresh, strict set rather than testing.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
